        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
Investigators sought to design, construct, and test an innovative, robust, low mass, low power separation device that can extract carbon dioxide (CO2) from respiratory gases in support of air revitalization for astronaut life support. The research will demonstrate the viability of the separation process by building a proof-of-concept CO2 separator. The separation device will be scaled to 1/8 of an astronaut. The research will focus on optimizing reactor performance, increasing catalyst lifetime, improving reactor design, modeling reactive transport and initiating process engineering designs towards constructing and delivering a demonstration reactor to NASA's Johnson Space Center (JSC) for later testing. 
<p>
The principal goal of this work was to elevate the status of the (HF-EBCLM) reactor from laboratory development stage to field test, i.e., to increase from TRL 1-3 to TRL 4-6. This will be accomplished by better understanding, controlling, and optimizing the design. The goals were to: 1. Demonstrate performance commensurate with EVA requirements. 2. CO2 permeance of 1.35 x 10-7 moles/m2 s Pa for a feed supply of 1% CO2. 3. CO2: O2 selectivity of > 2,800:1 2, and 4. Demonstrate lifetime of the reactor core for at least 6 months. The Objectives to be satisfied to achieve these goals were: 1. Coordinate three technical areas: (a) CLM chemistry, (b) reactor design, and (c) system engineering. This will be accomplished by: i. Finalize trade-off analysis for the composition of the contained liquid membrane and the enzyme concentration. ii. Complete the 1-dimensional reactive transport (1-DRT) modeling to predict reactor performance. iii. Continue the process engineering effort. 2. Work with NASA (JSC, KSC, ARC) to carry out ESM analysis. NASA JSC will provide in-kind support. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
Investigators 1) developed a model system to predict the preferred chemical makeup of the contained liquid membrane needed to maximize CO2 capture from very dilute feeds such as respiratory gas; 2) developed exhaustive mathematical models for both chemical reaction, known as equilibrium modeling, and mass transfer, known as rate-based modeling. The models were highly predictive and were validated by experimental measurement; 3) developed methods for enzyme immobilization in functionalized silica gel and by cross linked enzyme crystals. While these were more stable to denaturants than are solutions of native enzyme the attendant decrease in enzyme activity was significant enough to prevent their use; 4) developed methods to avoid use of UV sterilization by increasing salt content. While modest, this level is sufficient to prevent bacterial growth in the reactor, and 5) demonstrated stability of the reactor for a period of at least 5-days of continuous run. 
<p>
Overall system performance improved from 10% of that needed for EVA to about 50% and met certain goals for ALS applications.  The investigators carried out process engineering to initiate design of a piece of apparatus for EVA applications and designed a next generation to improve performance as much as 20-fold. 
<p>
        </div>

